{%- comment -%}
  Open Graph + JSON-LD. Parts get LocalBusiness context so a search for
  "alternator Amite LA" can connect the listing to the yard.

  shop.name and product.description arrive HTML-escaped ("A&amp;B MOTORS"). That is
  right for a meta tag, but a <script> body is raw text, so the entity survives the
  JSON string and Google indexes the literal "A&amp;B MOTORS". Liquid has no unescape
  filter, so undo the entities by hand — &amp; last, or "&amp;lt;" would decode twice.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign shop_name = shop.name | replace: '&#39;', "'" | replace: '&quot;', '"' | replace: '&gt;', '>' | replace: '&lt;', '<' | replace: '&amp;', '&'
  comment
    theme.liquid always passes a description and its own last resort is never blank, so the
    remaining fallbacks are belt-and-braces for a caller that renders this snippet without
    one. shop.description is deliberately not among them: it is the Online Store blurb that
    advertises used vehicles the yard does not sell.
  endcomment
  assign social_description = description | default: page_description | default: shop.name
-%}
<meta property="og:site_name" content="{{ shop.name }}">
<meta property="og:url" content="{{ canonical_url }}">
<meta property="og:title" content="{{ page_title | escape }}">
<meta property="og:type" content="{% if request.page_type == 'product' %}product{% else %}website{% endif %}">
<meta property="og:description" content="{{ social_description | strip_html | escape_once }}">
{%- comment -%}
  https, not http: the storefront is served over TLS, and Facebook/X reject or silently drop
  a mixed-content preview image. The JSON-LD below already had this right.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if request.page_type == 'product' and product.featured_media -%}
  <meta property="og:image" content="https:{{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 1200 }}">
  <meta property="og:image:alt" content="{{ product.title | escape }}">
  <meta property="og:price:amount" content="{{ product.price | divided_by: 100.0 }}">
  <meta property="og:price:currency" content="{{ cart.currency.iso_code }}">
{%- elsif settings.logo -%}
  <meta property="og:image" content="https:{{ settings.logo | image_url: width: 1200 }}">
  <meta property="og:image:alt" content="{{ shop.name | escape }}">
{%- endif -%}
<meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image">

{%- if request.page_type == 'product' -%}
  {%- liquid
    assign variant = product.selected_or_first_available_variant
    assign ld_description = product.description | strip_html | replace: '&#39;', "'" | replace: '&quot;', '"' | replace: '&gt;', '>' | replace: '&lt;', '<' | replace: '&amp;', '&' | truncate: 600
  -%}
  <script type="application/ld+json">
    {
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "Product",
      "name": {{ product.title | strip_html | json }},
      "url": {{ shop.url | append: product.url | json }},
      {%- comment -%}
        Only when the photograph is of the part. A donor-vehicle picture in `image` puts a
        whole car into a Google listing for a blower motor, which is a misrepresentation
        the shopper never sees coming — the product page discloses the donor photo, a
        search result does not.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- capture ld_photos -%}{%- render 'photo-source', product: product -%}{%- endcapture -%}
      {% if product.featured_media and ld_photos == 'exact' %}"image": [{{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 1200 | prepend: "https:" | json }}],{% endif %}
      "description": {{ ld_description | json }},
      "sku": {{ variant.sku | json }},
      {%- comment -%}
        M-21. brand was product.vendor — A&B Motors — while `seller` below is also A&B
        Motors, so the structured data claimed the yard manufactured the part it is
        reselling. For a used OEM part the brand is the vehicle manufacturer, which
        abm.fitment carries per application.

        Only asserted when every application agrees on one make. A third of the catalogue
        fits several makes (a mirror shared by KIA and Hyundai), and picking one of them
        would brand a Hyundai part KIA. An absent brand costs a rich-result field; a wrong
        one is a misrepresentation, so those omit it.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- liquid
        assign oem_brand = ''
        assign oem_conflict = false
        for row in product.metafields.abm.fitment.value
          assign row_make = row.make | strip
          if row_make != blank
            if oem_brand == ''
              assign oem_brand = row_make
            elsif oem_brand != row_make
              assign oem_conflict = true
            endif
          endif
        endfor
      -%}
      {%- if oem_brand != '' and oem_conflict == false -%}
      "brand": { "@type": "Brand", "name": {{ oem_brand | json }} },
      {%- endif -%}
      "itemCondition": "https://schema.org/UsedCondition",
      "offers": {
        "@type": "Offer",
        "price": "{{ variant.price | divided_by: 100.0 }}",
        "priceCurrency": {{ cart.currency.iso_code | json }},
        "availability": "{% if product.available %}https://schema.org/InStock{% else %}https://schema.org/OutOfStock{% endif %}",
        "url": {{ shop.url | append: product.url | json }},
        {%- comment -%}
          Google drops the price from a rich result once priceValidUntil lapses, and
          omitting it warns in Search Console. Every part is a one-off, so a rolling
          year is the honest answer: the price holds until the part sells.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        "priceValidUntil": "{{ 'now' | date: '%s' | plus: 31536000 | date: '%Y-%m-%d' }}",
        {%- comment -%}
          Straight off /policies/refund-policy: 90 days from delivery, by mail. The
          yard pays return shipping when the part is defective, misdescribed or
          damaged, but the buyer pays it when they simply changed their mind — so
          ReturnFeesCustomerResponsibility is the claim we can always stand behind.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        "hasMerchantReturnPolicy": {
          "@type": "MerchantReturnPolicy",
          "applicableCountry": "US",
          "returnPolicyCategory": "https://schema.org/MerchantReturnFiniteReturnWindow",
          "merchantReturnDays": {{ settings.warranty_days | default: 90 }},
          "returnMethod": "https://schema.org/ReturnByMail",
          "returnFees": "https://schema.org/ReturnFeesCustomerResponsibility"
        },
        {%- comment -%}
          Shipping mirrors the ship:* tag, which is what the delivery profile charges
          at checkout. Body panels and glass are never shipped, so they get no
          shippingDetails at all rather than a rate we would refuse to honour.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- comment -%}
          Both answers come from 'shipping-group', which is the file that owns the rates
          and the only one check_contracts.py holds against content/freight.json. Keeping a
          second table of rates here is exactly what went wrong before: it knew freight and
          two of the seven ground tiers, so the other five published shippingRate 0 and told
          Google that 11,000-odd paid listings shipped free.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- capture ship_rate -%}{%- render 'shipping-group', product: product, style: 'rate' -%}{%- endcapture -%}
        {%- capture ship_carrier -%}{%- render 'shipping-group', product: product, style: 'carrier' -%}{%- endcapture -%}
        {%- liquid
          assign ship_rate = ship_rate | strip
          assign ship_carrier = ship_carrier | strip
          assign handle_min = 1
          assign handle_max = 2
          assign transit_min = 2
          assign transit_max = 5
          assign ships = true
          if ship_carrier == 'pickup'
            assign ships = false
          endif
          comment
            Freight moves on a truck, not a parcel network, so both windows widen. A paid
            ground rate is still a parcel, so it keeps the parcel windows — the test is
            which carrier, not whether the shopper is charged.
          endcomment
          if ship_carrier == 'freight'
            assign handle_min = 2
            assign handle_max = 4
            assign transit_min = 3
            assign transit_max = 7
          endif
        -%}
        {%- if ships -%}
        "shippingDetails": {
          "@type": "OfferShippingDetails",
          "shippingRate": {
            "@type": "MonetaryAmount",
            "value": "{{ ship_rate }}",
            "currency": {{ cart.currency.iso_code | json }}
          },
          {%- comment -%}
            M-22. This said addressCountry US, which promises the rate applies in Alaska
            and Hawaii — where the policy says to call for a quote. Naming the contiguous
            states plus DC is the accurate form, and it is the one Google reads: a shopper
            in Anchorage is told the flat rate is not theirs before checkout rather than
            after. 'lower 48' in the copy and this list are the same claim.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          "shippingDestination": {
            "@type": "DefinedRegion",
            "addressCountry": "US",
            "addressRegion": [
              "AL","AZ","AR","CA","CO","CT","DE","DC","FL","GA","ID","IL","IN","IA","KS",
              "KY","LA","ME","MD","MA","MI","MN","MS","MO","MT","NE","NV","NH","NJ","NM",
              "NY","NC","ND","OH","OK","OR","PA","RI","SC","SD","TN","TX","UT","VT","VA",
              "WA","WV","WI","WY"
            ]
          },
          "deliveryTime": {
            "@type": "ShippingDeliveryTime",
            "handlingTime": {
              "@type": "QuantitativeValue",
              "minValue": {{ handle_min }}, "maxValue": {{ handle_max }}, "unitCode": "DAY"
            },
            "transitTime": {
              "@type": "QuantitativeValue",
              "minValue": {{ transit_min }}, "maxValue": {{ transit_max }}, "unitCode": "DAY"
            }
          }
        },
        {%- endif -%}
        "seller": { "@type": "AutoPartsStore", "name": {{ shop_name | json }} }
      }
    }
  </script>

  {%- comment -%}
    Mirrors the breadcrumb the page actually renders, so the trail Google shows
    matches the one a visitor can click. Both come out of product-crumb, which is
    the only place the rungs are worked out.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <script type="application/ld+json">
    {
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      "itemListElement": [
{% render 'product-crumb', format: 'json', product: product, collection: collection %}
      ]
    }
  </script>
{%- endif -%}

{%- if request.page_type == 'index' -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    sameAs is how Google connects this website to the same business it already knows
    from eBay, Facebook and the map listing. Each one is a setting, so a link that
    changes never needs a code change — and an empty setting drops out of the array
    rather than publishing an empty string.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- liquid
    assign profiles = ''
    if settings.ebay_url != blank
      assign profiles = profiles | append: settings.ebay_url | append: '~'
    endif
    if settings.facebook_url != blank
      assign profiles = profiles | append: settings.facebook_url | append: '~'
    endif
    if settings.gbp_url != blank
      assign profiles = profiles | append: settings.gbp_url | append: '~'
    endif
    assign profiles = profiles | split: '~'
  -%}
  <script type="application/ld+json">
    {
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "AutoPartsStore",
      "name": {{ shop_name | json }},
      "url": {{ shop.url | json }},
      {% if settings.logo %}"logo": {{ settings.logo | image_url: width: 600 | prepend: "https:" | json }},
      "image": {{ settings.logo | image_url: width: 1200 | prepend: "https:" | json }},{% endif %}
      "telephone": {{ settings.phone | json }},
      "email": {{ settings.email | json }},
      {%- if settings.established != blank %}
      "foundingDate": {{ settings.established | json }},{% endif %}
      "priceRange": "$$",
      "address": {
        "@type": "PostalAddress",
        "streetAddress": "59174 Hwy 51",
        "addressLocality": "Amite",
        "addressRegion": "LA",
        "postalCode": "70422",
        "addressCountry": "US"
      },
      {%- comment -%}
        Both halves or neither. A coordinate with one side missing puts the yard on
        the equator, which is worse than publishing no coordinate at all.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if settings.geo_lat != blank and settings.geo_lng != blank %}
      "geo": {
        "@type": "GeoCoordinates",
        "latitude": {{ settings.geo_lat | json }},
        "longitude": {{ settings.geo_lng | json }}
      },{% endif %}
      {%- comment -%}
        The towns the yard actually delivers to free, straight off the shipping page.
        Claiming a wider area than the truck goes is the kind of thing Google's local
        results and an annoyed customer both catch.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      "areaServed": [
        { "@type": "AdministrativeArea", "name": "Tangipahoa Parish, Louisiana" },
        { "@type": "City", "name": "Amite, LA" },
        { "@type": "City", "name": "Hammond, LA" },
        { "@type": "City", "name": "Independence, LA" },
        { "@type": "City", "name": "Kentwood, LA" },
        { "@type": "City", "name": "Ponchatoula, LA" },
        { "@type": "City", "name": "Roseland, LA" },
        { "@type": "City", "name": "Tickfaw, LA" }
      ],
      {%- if profiles.size > 0 %}
      "sameAs": [{% for url in profiles %}{{ url | json }}{% unless forloop.last %}, {% endunless %}{% endfor %}],{% endif %}
      "openingHours": "Mo-Fr 08:00-17:00",
      "openingHoursSpecification": {
        "@type": "OpeningHoursSpecification",
        "dayOfWeek": ["Monday", "Tuesday", "Wednesday", "Thursday", "Friday"],
        "opens": "08:00",
        "closes": "17:00"
      }
    }
  </script>
{%- endif -%}
